1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,336
Program the harbor gate to an interface
and the JDK already knows how to wrap it.
java.lang.reflect.Proxy implements that
interface at runtime and funnels every
call through one InvocationHandler.

2
00:00:12,455 --> 00:00:28,295
Spring AOP leans on that when an
interface-based proxy is enough. No
subclass of your concrete class. A brand-
new type that only promises the interface
methods. Interface-oriented design and JDK
proxies fit cleanly.

3
00:00:28,415 --> 00:00:35,063
You inject GateService, not
HarborGateService, and Spring can
substitute a proxy that still is-a
GateService.

4
00:00:35,183 --> 00:00:46,727
Callers never need the concrete class —
which is exactly what the yard crane
console and the trucker API both depend on
when metrics and transactions wrap
release. Look at this java for a moment.

5
00:00:46,847 --> 00:00:55,679
Look at this java for a moment. Walk a
release call at runtime. Collaborators
hold a field of type GateService.

6
00:00:55,799 --> 00:01:05,951
After context refresh with matching
advisors, the injected object is often a
JDK proxy implementing GateService (and
possibly other interfaces the target
exposes),

7
00:01:06,072 --> 00:01:11,256
holding HarborGateService as the target
inside Spring's AOP infrastructure.

8
00:01:11,376 --> 00:01:21,072
The trucker API calls
gateService.release(cargoId). The call
enters the proxy's InvocationHandler, not
HarborGateService directly.

9
00:01:21,192 --> 00:01:29,304
The interceptor chain runs — here the
metrics @Around records nanos, and any
transactional advisor would begin/commit
around proceed.

10
00:01:29,424 --> 00:01:40,248
pjp.proceed() eventually reflects into
HarborGateService.release, which loads
cargo and opens the boom. Return value
flows back through the same chain.

11
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:50,880
The concrete class is not the type of the
injected field — and that is fine for
every caller that programmed to the
interface. Failure mode that shows up in
code review and production.

12
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:06,288
Someone injects or casts to
HarborGateService while Spring published a
JDK proxy. The proxy is not an instance of
HarborGateService. It implements the
interface only. instanceof
HarborGateService is false.

13
00:02:06,408 --> 00:02:18,648
(HarborGateService) gateService throws
ClassCastException at the edge that
assumed the impl type — often a @Autowired
HarborGateService field that fails at
injection with an unsatisfied dependency
or a wrong-type match d

14
00:02:18,768 --> 00:02:21,960
epending on how candidates were filtered.

15
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:33,192
Self-invocation inside HarborGateService —
this.status(gateId) from release — never
enters the proxy, so advice on status does
not run for that path; metrics and
transactions skip.

16
00:02:33,312 --> 00:02:40,296
Pointcuts written against the concrete
class name may not match the way you
expect when the public entry is the
interface proxy.

17
00:02:40,416 --> 00:02:51,312
Final methods on the concrete class are
irrelevant to the JDK proxy type because
callers should not see the concrete class
through the proxy reference — only
interface methods are dispatched through
the handler.

18
00:02:51,432 --> 00:03:04,440
JDK proxies can implement multiple
interfaces. If HarborGateService also
implements HealthIndicator, the proxy can
expose both, and injectors typed to either
interface may receive the same proxy
instance.

19
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:14,112
Only interface methods are advised through
this mechanism. Package-private or
protected methods on the impl are not part
of the proxy's public contract.

20
00:03:14,232 --> 00:03:23,184
Selection rule of thumb: when the target
implements at least one interface (other
than marker-only cases Spring may ignore),
JDK proxy is eligible.

21
00:03:23,304 --> 00:03:35,496
Force class-based proxies with
spring.aop.proxy-target-class=true or
@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(proxyTargetClass =
true) and you push toward CGLIB even when
interfaces exist.

22
00:03:35,616 --> 00:03:42,528
Boot defaults have shifted over versions —
verify what you actually get instead of
assuming a textbook JDK proxy.

23
00:03:42,648 --> 00:03:56,568
Debug with
AopUtils.isJdkDynamicProxy(bean) or glance
at the class name — $Proxy12 is a clue you
are in interface-proxy land;
$$SpringCGLIB$$ means class proxy. Trade-
offs.

24
00:03:56,688 --> 00:04:06,144
JDK proxies need no bytecode subclassing
of your concrete type, play well with
final classes that still implement
interfaces, and keep the API surface
honest.

25
00:04:06,264 --> 00:04:11,880
They punish concrete-type injection and
are unavailable when there is no interface
to implement.

26
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,088
Prefer interface injection everywhere gate
collaborators are wired; reserve concrete
types for the @Service implementation
class itself.

27
00:04:20,208 --> 00:04:33,960
Misconception unique to JDK proxies: "The
proxy is a subclass of HarborGateService."
It is not. It is a sibling stand-in that
shares the interface, generated by
Proxy.newProxyInstance.

28
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:46,608
Another misconception: "If I have an
interface, Boot always uses JDK proxies."
Configuration and Boot version may choose
CGLIB anyway — check before writing casts
or instanceof against the impl.

29
00:04:46,728 --> 00:05:03,360
The limitation is the next engineering
wall. What if there is no interface? What
if the bean is a concrete @Service
injected by its class type? JDK Proxy
cannot subclass that concrete type. You
need CGLIB.
